// Practice quiz: Bean Validation on requests
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/validation-errors-and-testing/bean-validation-on-requests

::o1 trigger::What triggers validation of a @RequestBody parameter? {
~@RestController#Not quite. That only sets response writing.
=@Valid on the parameter#Correct. Without it, the constraints are not applied to the body.
~@Entity#Not quite. That is about persistence.
~@Bean#Not quite. That registers objects.
}

::o1 not blank::Which constraint rejects a string that is only spaces? {
~@NotNull#Not quite. A string of spaces is not null.
~@NotEmpty#Not quite. A string of spaces is not empty.
~@Positive#Not quite. That is for numbers.
=@NotBlank#Correct. It needs at least one non-whitespace character.
}

::o2 status::A body fails validation at a @Valid @RequestBody parameter. What status does the client get by default? {
~200#Not quite. The handler does not run.
~404#Not quite. The path matched.
~500#Not quite. The server did not fail.
=400#Correct. It is a client error.
}

::o2 several failures::A body breaks two constraints. How many failures does validation report? {
=Both#Correct. It collects all failures.
~Only the first#Not quite. It does not stop at the first.
~Only the last#Not quite. It reports them all.
~None, because they cancel out#Not quite. They do not cancel out.
}

::o3 parameter constraint::How do you validate a path variable to be positive? {
~Add @Valid to the class only#Not quite. A constraint must be declared on the value.
~Use @RequestBody#Not quite. That is for bodies.
=Put @Positive on the parameter#Correct. Spring applies method validation.
~Use a database constraint only#Not quite. That fails far later, and differently.
}

